    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Local winds are localized atmospheric circulations driven by regional variations in temperature and atmospheric pressure.",
        "Chinook (North America) and Foehn (Alps) are dry, warm downslope winds that rapidly melt snow and raise regional temperatures.",
        "Harmattan is a dry, dust-laden wind blowing off the Sahara towards West Africa, historically known as 'The Doctor' for its drying effect.",
        "Loo is an extremely hot, dry summer wind characteristic of the Northern Plains of India and Pakistan.",
        "Mistral and Bora are prominent examples of cold katabatic local winds affecting Southern Europe and the Mediterranean basin."
      ],
      "id": "o0krl44"
    },
    {
